---
import { getChapters, splitChapter } from '../lib/chapter-order';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const chapters = await getChapters();
---

<Layout title="Learn Me a Threat">
  <h1>Learn Me a Threat</h1>

  <aside class="callout callout-note">
    <p>
      This blog is still very much a work in progress. If this is your
      first time here and you're interested, maybe come back in a few
      weeks when there's more content.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <section class="about">
    <p>
      I'm Zach. I just graduated grad school with a Master's degree in
      Physics, where I spent the last few years studying how quantum
      computers threaten Bitcoin. Along the way, I picked up a decent sense
      of how quantum computers affect other cryptocurrencies too.
    </p>
    <p>
      I kind of stumbled into this. I started my Master's mostly because
      quantum computing was hyped up at the time and I was curious about it
      after some exposure to it in undergrad research. When it came time to
      pick a thesis topic, I landed on quantum attacks against Bitcoin for
      two reasons. First, it's a direct application of quantum computing that anyone
      can actually understand, not some esoteric thing only other physicists
      would get the point of. Also, crypto and NFTs were huge at the time,
      like Bored Ape NFTs selling for millions of dollars, so that definitely
      played into it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I knew basically nothing about crypto or Bitcoin going in, but the more
      I read the more I got hooked on one specific thing about it. You're
      sending money back and forth to total strangers, with no bank in the
      middle and nobody you can call if something goes wrong. You'd think
      that shouldn't work at all, since nobody in it trusts anybody. It does
      work though, because the trust is created artificially, from nothing
      but math. I thought that was super interesting, and it's basically
      what turned into my thesis, which turned into a paper that's on <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.23952">arXiv</a> now and in the
      process of getting published.
    </p>
    <p>
      Honestly, I really like yapping about this stuff, and now that I'm done
      with school I don't have an outlet for that anymore. I find the topic interesting and I like
      teaching it. Some of my fondest memories from grad school are from
      teaching, as long as it was on something I actually cared about. This
      blog is meant to bring that feeling back.
    </p>
    <p>
      So think of this blog as a museum of my thoughts while they're still
      fresh. If you want to follow along and find it interesting, that's a
      nice bonus. I do think it's useful on its own, though. How quantum
      computers affect cryptocurrencies and blockchains is a broader topic
      than most people expect, and the information is scattered across a
      pile of papers and reports. I want to put as much of it in one place as
      I can. I won't cover every attack or edge case, since there are too
      many, but I want to give a real lay of the land: how each attack works,
      what people think about it, and what could be (or is being) done.
    </p>
    <p>
      You don't need any prior knowledge of Bitcoin, cryptography, or
      quantum computing. My goal is to make this accessible to anyone,
      explaining things rigorously from the ground up, in as plain language
      as I can manage.
    </p>
    <p>
      This blog, and its name, are inspired by <a
      href="https://learnmeabitcoin.com">learnmeabitcoin.com</a> by Greg
      Walker. It does a great job explaining how Bitcoin works in plain
      language. I won't go nearly as deep into Bitcoin's mechanics here
      because that's not really the point. If that's what you're after, go
      check out Greg's site.
    </p>
    <p>
      Last thing, a disclaimer. I dislike writing and I'm slow at it, which
      I know sounds a bit paradoxical for someone writing a blog. I like this stuff
      more than I dislike writing about it, though, so writing is just the
      tax I pay to get it out of my head. I use AI (mainly Claude) to help
      with that part. I write out my raw thoughts and ideas, then use AI to
      help with structure, clarity, and flow, and I check accuracy along the
      way. The ideas, rigor, and explanations are mine, but parts of this
      blog are AI-assisted. If that's a dealbreaker, no hard feelings.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 class="contents-heading">Contents</h2>

  {chapters.size === 0 ? (
    <div class="empty-state">
      <h2>No posts yet.</h2>
      <p>Check back soon.</p>
    </div>
  ) : (
    [...chapters].map(([chapterName, chapterPosts]) => {
      const { sectionPost, subsectionPosts } = splitChapter(chapterPosts);
      return (
        <section class="chapter">
          <h2 class="chapter-label">{chapterName}</h2>
          {sectionPost ? (
            <>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li>
                  <a class="post-title" href={`/posts/${sectionPost.data.slug}/`}>{sectionPost.data.title}</a>
                </li>
              </ul>
              {subsectionPosts.length > 0 && (
                <ul class="subsection-list">
                  {subsectionPosts.map((post) => (
                    <li>
                      <a class="post-title" href={`/posts/${post.data.slug}/`}>{post.data.title}</a>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              )}
            </>
          ) : (
            <ul class="post-list">
            {chapterPosts.map((post) => (
              <li>
                <a class="post-title" href={`/posts/${post.data.slug}/`}>{post.data.title}</a>
              </li>
            ))}
            </ul>
          )}
        </section>
      );
    })
  )}
</Layout>

<style>
  .contents-heading {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .post-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .post-title {
    font-size: 24px;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .chapter-label {
    font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm);
  }

  .chapter + .chapter {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .subsection-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-md);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }
</style>
